nt to take this opportunity to  acknowledge our educators parents and  students for their dedication to making  the return to in-person learning as  successful and as safe as possible  in the last week approximately 13.3  percent of students and staff were  absent each day and over 94 percent of  schools have reported less than 30  percent of students and staff absent  we also saw less than one percent of  schools were closed each day  so only seven out of four thousand eight  hundred and forty four schools  and these closures are typically due to  operational challenges such such as  staff shortages  keeping our children and youth in school  is critical to their mental health and  well-being and i thank you for all  the support you're doing for our  children's academic success  in addition to all the measures in place  at schools to help them  remain as safe as possible vaccination  is also a key and the vaccines we have  are safe and effective and the best way  to protect our children from kovid 19.  to date 87.1 percent of ontarians age 12  to 17 have received one dose of a covet  19 vaccine and 83.6 percent are fully  vaccinated and we continue to make  progress in our vaccination campaign for  our five to 11 year olds  53.9 percent have received one dose and  20.2 percent are fully immunized we  increase that by 10 percent in january  alone so thank you to the parents  as we seek to improve our immunization  rates in our youngest eligible age group  it is our goal to ensure that parents of  children feel well informed and safe  when deciding to have their children  immunized  the children's health coalition in  collaboration with the hospital for sick  children has launched a children's  vaccine confidence campaign to provide  parents and caregivers with information  to make an informed decision  parents caregivers and children are also  encouraged to call the provincial  vaccine confidence line or book a  confidential phone appointment with sick  kids clinician through their covet 19  vaccine consultation service  i know that for parents  nothing is more important than the  health and well-being  of their children and have incredible  sources of information they can rely on  to be scientific and factually correct  is important  the resources are there to help you  answer all the questions you may have  as are your trusted health care  professionals  jose  though vaccination remains key we do  have antivirals available to help us  manage the impact of this virus  because of the limited supply of  paxlovid which is an outpatient oral  treatment we are prioritizing the  distribution to unvaccinated individuals  who are at risk of severe outcomes of  covid19 infection including  hospitalization  this includes unvaccinated seniors aged  60 and over  first nations inuit and metis  individuals age 50 and over  and individuals age 50 and over with  underlying risk factors  we are also prioritizing  immunocompromised individuals 18 and  over regardless of their vaccine status  as of today 12  treatment courses of paxlovit have been  distributed through the 15 sites  though  it is no substitute for immunization  paxlovid is available and will  significantly reduce the risk of  hospitalization if taken within five  days  of symptom onset and in the randomized  controlled trials they reduce the risk  by 88 percent  if you think you may be eligible to  receive pacslovid you can contact your  primary care provider  call telehealth ontario or visit a  covid19 clinical assessment center  community  i also wanted to mention an update on  our pcr testing eligibility we continue  to prioritize pcr testing for those at  the highest risk of severe outcomes  including those eligible for covet 19  treatments such as antivirals those who  work and live in the highest risk  settings and patient facing health care  workers we are expanding eligibility to  now include household members  of patient-facing health care workers in  order to support timely identification  of cases in healthcare workers in  ongoing delivery of healthcare services  i want to thank ontarians for the  sacrifices they have made and for their  continued cooperation when it comes to  following the public health measures  that we know  work  your actions are having an impact the  trends are looking  positive but we must remain cautious and  our continued reopening efforts must be  implemented cautiously thank you merci  we'll go to the phone lines for  questions just a reminder it's one  question one follow-up over to the first  question please  from allison jones at the canadian press  hi dr moore  um  given the significant number of people  who were infected with omicron over the  past two months i think a lot of people  are curious about what sort of immunity  that confers  if you were fully vaccinated and got  omicron or even boosted and got omicron  can you  throw caution to the wind  assume you've got super immunity or are  people who had omicron susceptible to  being re-infected with omicron i think  there's some preliminary research out  there but is ontario tracking this  so a really good question um it it  appears with omicron if you get natural  immunity so you're not vaccinated and  you get infected by omicron  you do have some immunity against  further reinfection but there is  significantly a risk for re-infection  from omicron over time  so we're not confident in how long that  duration of immunity is from natural  exposure yet it's still early days we  have heard from our immunologist  partners  that if you have had natural infection  and you get an mrna booster vaccine that  significantly adds to your level of  protection and  length of protection against omicron  so it has been the recommendation from  the national advisory committee to  get immunized with that mrna vaccine  even if you've had natural exposure  given that it provides longer  and stronger and more beneficial  protection against any risk for  re-infection so you may and layman's  turns consider getting infected  naturally by omicron as  almost equal to one dose of vaccine  and subsequent benefit  is uh maintained by having a follow-up  vaccine uh  obviously after complete resolution of  symptoms and it's best to actually have  a you know a roughly eight-week minimum  time delay um from your first exposure  that will give you much longer  lasting and acting protection  so pretty complex question but thanks  for asking it  um yeah  a slightly different topic you and some  other health officials and government  officials have started talking recently  about learning to live with covet and  i'm wondering if you could give us a  fuller picture of what that looks like  does it mean just returning to our 2019  way of life or does it mean something  else  yeah i think we really have to  approach  the reality that omicron is highly  infectious and is going to be difficult  to control and limit  and now that we have safe and effective  vaccines  as well as safe and effective outpatient  treatments and inpatient treatments it  comes  to a time where we're going to have to  decide how many public health measures  we'll maintain going forward  and  how we can try to maintain our mental  physical social economic and educational  well-being in the face  of an ongoing threat from this strain of  viruses  i think it's a discussion we have to  have at a societal level of what our  values are  but with these tools that we now have in  place i think the the discussion has to  occur  and we have to decide as a society how  many public health measures we want to  just recommend and or maintain in a  legal fashion to limit the spread of the  viruses i think that discussion should  happen soon given that  some of our trending is heading in the  right direction we're following other  countries such as the uk south africa  that are also on downward trends from  omicron  and yet don't have the same level of  public health measures in place so it'll  be a societal discussion um  and government will make the eventual  decisions  but i think it's worthwhile  as a society to start having those  discussions  next question  from  randall denley at the national post  hello dr mark  i  looking at the  [Music]  vaccine dashboard and i know that the  case levels between  comparing the vaccinated unvaccinated  are quite similar to each other  i'm also seeing some international  studies saying that the  similar rates of transmission between  the two groups i wonder what your  thoughts are now did you unvaccinate  still pose  a greater threat  of transmission than the vaccinated in  ontario  so well  really complex question um  the  two doses of vaccine  with omicron don't seem to limit the  risk of transmission significantly  and  though three doses can give you a much  better protection against the risk of  transmission  so up to 60 percent uh benefit on  decreasing your risk of transmission so  the the problem is now that the this  vaccine that we have against omicron  doesn't um cause sterile immunity  doesn't limit the risk of an immunized  person carrying the virus significantly  with two doses three doses certainly  does  i should say though the major caveat and  protection of the vaccine is that it  limits the severe outcomes in recent  studies that are going to get published  from ontario data show  up to 95 percent protection of three  doses against severe outcomes associated  with this virus so  your very good point the vaccine isn't  providing significant benefit at two  doses against the risk of transmission  uh as compared to someone unvaccinated  but it provides excellent benefit and  what i think most people want is to  avoid hospitalization and ending up in  the intensive care unit or frankly dying  from this virus  the vaccine protects significantly  against those severe outcomes um  so when when we see a vaccine that  doesn't provide the sterile immunity  like  it did provide against delta we have to  reassess  some of the public health measures that  we put in place reassess  whether  we maintain a proof of vaccination  certification process  uh given the decreased benefit against  transmission but reiterating three doses  does provide a significant benefit  follow-up  i  anticipated my second question i was  going to ask you whether that  uh the difference between the two is  enough now to justify a  passport system although we have one in  place apparently until  march the 15th but let me rephrase it  because i think you said for two doses  no if i hear you correctly but  is your sense that we should keep  passports and go with three doses  or get rid of passwords i think we have  to reassess the value of the passports  in the coming  weeks and months and clearly again  that's a government decision the the  main benefit of the passport system now  is that against severe outcomes uh we  are well protected with two doses uh and  hence increased socialization of someone  unvaccinated in increasing their risk of  getting omicron through accessing any of  these venues does increase their  personal risk so it does protect the  unvaccinated person  well against  transmission and  against severe outcomes  by not being able to access mass  gatherings and or many of these venues  but  my preference would be that we only have  6.7 million ontarians that taken  advantage of the third dose is that more  ontarians especially if you've got any  chronic underlying illness  and or  by age alone 50 and up  i would really suggest you get that  third dose if you haven't already it is  proving to be so beneficial against  the risk of transmission but also  against  severe outcomes  so  we just have to be honest about the  benefits now of two doses of the vaccine  and review the implications for all of  our public health measures that we put  in place  and i think those discussions will will  be happening in the coming weeks and  months  as you've raised russell  next question  from matthew bingley at the global news  hi doctor i'd just like to follow up on  allison's question uh you you said that  you know some of those decisions would  have to be uh made at a societal level  of of what measures might need to be  considered going forward i'm wondering  what options you would actually suggest  in in your capacity as as  public health measures that that would  be necessary to keep around for a while  so the general recommendations  on distancing and hand hygiene  i think  are prudent and reasonable  but  clearly  the most important one  and probably the last one to go will be  masking  given that masking protects  me it protects you uh but it protects  the most vulnerable members of our  community from transmission and and i  think that will be probably the last of  the public health measures to go um we  just had a brief discussion about the  proof of vaccination three doses is so  much superior to two doses against risk  of transmission uh and severe outcomes  that i hope more ontarians embrace it um  yeah but we're awaiting federal guidance  on whether um their proof of vaccination  for international travel will move to  three vaccines etc um but in in the  interim uh of all the measures uh uh i  think the masking will be the last to go  we'll certainly have to review the proof  of vaccination uh i think it's common  sense uh on  hand hygiene and distancing um  uh and i would hope even if we don't  mandate masking that anyone that's  vulnerable um to this virus will  continue to mask and that it become a  community standard especially  as we return into the fall where we  anticipate uh all the respiratory  viruses will try to come back and spread  amongst us  follow-up  thank you and on  ba2 uh with the number of um cases that  have been seen to take over in denmark  with with our reopening i'm wondering  uh  how many cases you're currently tracking  in the province and and what concerns  you have as as more people begin to go  out and  uh  it has the opportunity to spread more  here  well great question it's still early  days with ba2 we've had 29  cases of  ba2 detected in ontario through our  robust whole genome sequencing  strategy  those were reported to us  yesterday um clinically we're not seeing  any different outcomes it's just very  small numbers between  ba2 and others in terms of the  hospitalization rate of those 29 cases  or severe outcomes  it's something i think internationally  we're going to need large numbers to be  able to ascertain if there's an increase  in virulence we do anticipate that  there's an increase in transmissibility  of ba2  we will be monitoring about vaccine  effectiveness against it but there's  nothing from the our immunologists or  public health ontario scientists that  have told us that they're concerned  about vaccine escape with this strain  and given that we have good cellular  immunity against  omicron we anticipate having the same  against the ba2  strain of it  so it's early days we're monitoring the  situation very closely um that 29 cases  is an underrepresentation given that  it's the the whole genome sequencing  of the level of activity in our  community um and we'll be monitoring it  very closely going forward  next question  from stella dupree radio  hello dr moore  so i have a question for you you said  that we have to start learning to live  with kovid and i was wondering what does  that look like because we lived with  where we must masks staying home for  extended period of time with a runny  nose standing six feet apart  when does that end because that's not  how we live with other viruses  yeah it'll be a journey for us um  stella that's a great question we've had  two years where we've been very fearful  of this virus uh and and the strains  have changed over time uh we're we're  fortunate that with omicron it has less  severe outcomes than the previous  strains  and we're fortunate that we have safe  and effective vaccines that prevent  severe outcomes and now we have  good oral outpatient antiviral therapies  so as a society um to decrease that fear  will take time  for us to normalize living with this  virus  i i think some of the basic tenets that  we've learned over the last two years we  will maintain especially going into the  fall of hand hygiene and distancing many  of us will continue to wear masks even  if they're not mandated at that time  and i i do believe that  over time we won't have proof of  immunization  necessary to to get into certain venues  clearly the federal government has  responsibilities for international  travel and whether that will be  maintained but all of that has to get  reviewed  i would think the same tenants that if  you do have symptoms of a virus no  matter what virus a fever a cough or  runny nose and certainly there's  hundreds of different viruses that can  cause those symptoms that you would stay  home if you're ill  that you'd come back to work once your  symptoms have recovered  and that once at work you'd still  practice good hand hygiene monitoring  your symptoms etc so it'll be a journey  as a society to come out of this  and to normalize  omicron and covid as we've normalized  all the other viruses  and i do think we'll also have the  addition of an annual vaccine for the  next several years  just to maintain  immune protection against any surges of  hospitalization  and i'm looking forward to hearing from  the immunization companies whether  that'll get integrated with an influenza  vaccine which would certainly make it  easier from a patient vantage point  follow-up  uh yes dr moore i was wondering do you  consider that the worst of the pandemic  is behind us  i've been humbled by this virus too many  times we're in wave five now and alpha  beta gamma delta and now omicron  we know this virus wants to mutate  roughly two significant mutations  every month  and any one of them can emerge and  become a dominant strain  so i  hopeful i'm very hopeful about the  impact of the vaccine campaign as well  as the oral outpatient antiviral  therapies we now have available to us  that we can get back  to a more normal lifestyle but will be  have to be very cautious  we are monitoring the situation  internationally with our federal  partners looking  and adhering to  our surveillance obligations to detect  any new strains within ontario  and sharing that federally and then  internationally and and likewise i'm  confident all countries around the globe  are trying to identify if there's new  strains developing and taking  appropriate public health actions so  we're not out of the woods yet we still  have to be cautious um but we've learned  significantly from the last two years  and i do believe we're in a much better  position to learn to live with this  virus and to be less fearful of it  last question  from john mcgrath that's pvo  uh good afternoon doctor  in terms of the testing i i note that  you've expanded the eligibility for pcr  testing today but  you know  the the science table for example says  you know it's very challenging to do  modeling right now because the testing  is so limited we don't have an estimate  of what the the province-wide our number  is um  you know the the lack of testing really  is is hampering some of what we can say  we know  about uh what is going on in the  province right now and so i'm curious  why we aren't talking about a more  um  aggressive approach to to  expanding testing again uh if for no  other reason than to get a better idea  of what's going on well thanks john so  the pcr testing capacity in ontario is a  finite resource and we have to use it  judiciously we've also complemented the  pcr capacity with a very robust uh rapid  antigen testing capacity and  we and hopefully the supply will improve  from the federal government where we can  have have it even more widely available  uh 3.9 million rits distributed to  students across our elementary and  secondary schools that will be expanded  uh  and  replenished over time we continue to  have rapid androgen testing availability  to our long-term care partners  acute care partners as well for  screening for workers  and education workers as well so we're  using multiple different testing  modalities  that are complementary but i have to  acknowledge the pcr testing is a finite  capacity that we have to use judiciously  and we're just now getting our  turnaround time  to uh for within 48 hours  to to an acceptable 80 percent level  where we can action them because we need  to use pcr tests  in the high-risk congregate settings  hospital settings to help us  document who should and can receive  therapies because they're dependent upon  a pcr test being positive  so bear with us we're reviewing the  capacity on a regular basis the testing  expert panel gives us advice  on how we can judiciously slowly expand  the use  of the pcr capacity that we built in  ontario  and  i i think we should acknowledge we've  got good data coming from our hospitals  now on the total number of people  hospitalized which is on the decline  total number of people in the intensive  care unit  which is again on the decline  and the wastewater  surveillance system that is in  across ontario is showing also a decline  so we have multiple surveillance and  data points that are helping us give us  situational awareness  and from my vantage point monitoring all  of these we're trending in the right  direction we know reopening the economy  could lead to some  rough times in february as we have  increased socialization and  increased risk trends to transmission  but we'll monitor that and we've built  good hospital capacity for february so i  think january was tough  february will be better i do think see  things improving further in march  and then hopefully a low  endemic rate in april all the meanwhile  i hope ontarians come forward and  continue to take advantage of our robust  immunization strategy continue to take  advantage of the third doses which are  so protective against severe outcomes  i'd love more ontarians to come forward  for that third dose 84 days after your  last  to get the best protection we can from  these safe and effective vaccines  follow-up and this is the last question  uh there were i guess problems with you  know relying too much on the the daily  case count but it was it was the most  leading indicator that we had in terms  of warning us when a spike in  hospitalizations was coming so  do you see  things like the wastewater surveillance  as a substitute for that as a sort of an  early warning system or is are we going  to have to  expand pcr testing in a way that if we  don't have wide open eligibility is  there a way to  expand it in a way that restores  a bit more of a maybe a  statistical sample if you know what i  mean  that's a really good point so  we to be clear with 30 000 pcr tests  done on average last week uh percent  positivity is on the decline so to me it  that's a good  signal it's reliable now that it's a  stable population that's getting tested  i think the uh the wastewater  surveillance at a population level gives  us a good indication as well since so  many communities are seeing a decline in  the in the positivity and and then we're  what we traditionally do for respiratory  viruses is we have sentinel primary care  providers or sentinel uh uh  partners uh that tests every third or  fifth patient that show up with a  respiratory  complaint  of a virus like nature and we sample  them from multiple different viruses to  get awareness of what's the predominant  virus so outside of covid right now it's  respiratory syncytial virus is is the  significant uh strain that's  spreading across ontario uh we need to  have a a a robust sentinel surveillance  network that will also provide us with  input  on what are the circulating viruses and  we're working on having that up and  running for the this fall  so we can inform people of what's the  dominant virus so that they're aware and  they can take appropriate precautions  some spread more rapidly through  touching surfaces some  are more airborne and some are a mix  and we'll be able to communicate that  risk as schools reopen in the fall  and we move indoors in the in the winter  of 2022-23  thanks everyone

